1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:05,995
On one of the most remote islands on Earth stand nearly a thousand stone giants.

2
00:00:05,995 --> 00:00:08,968
Some are taller than a three-storey house.

3
00:00:08,968 --> 00:00:11,800
Some weigh as much as a dozen elephants.

4
00:00:12,266 --> 00:00:21,500
They were carved in one volcanic crater, then moved for kilometres across the island, with no wheels, no metal, and no draft animals.

5
00:00:21,966 --> 00:00:27,620
For more than fifty years, one bestselling answer has been: they didn't do it.

6
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:29,070
Stranded aliens did.

7
00:00:29,070 --> 00:00:34,000
Beings with advanced tools and a way of working stone unknown to us.

8
00:00:34,466 --> 00:00:38,542
The people of Rapa Nui have always given a different answer.

9
00:00:38,542 --> 00:00:44,451
Ask how the statues crossed the island, and the old stories say something very strange.

10
00:00:44,451 --> 00:00:45,266
They walked.

11
00:00:45,733 --> 00:00:48,820
So today we follow one statue on its journey.

12
00:00:48,820 --> 00:00:53,760
From the quarry, down the road, to the platform, and to what came after.

13
00:00:53,760 --> 00:00:57,533
At every station, the alien claim meets the experiment.

14
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,977
Station one is the quarry, Rano Raraku.

15
00:01:04,977 --> 00:01:12,000
About ninety-five percent of all moai were carved from the soft rock of this single volcano.

16
00:01:12,466 --> 00:01:15,324
The rock is tuff, compressed volcanic ash.

17
00:01:15,324 --> 00:01:25,666
Carvers outlined a figure on its back in the cliff face, then chipped around it until only a thin keel of stone along the spine held it to the mountain.

18
00:01:26,133 --> 00:01:29,559
Their tools are still lying there by the thousand.

19
00:01:29,559 --> 00:01:36,000
Basalt hand picks called toki, harder than the tuff, resharpened by knocking off a fresh edge.

20
00:01:36,466 --> 00:01:38,574
Here is the first alien claim.

21
00:01:38,574 --> 00:01:52,066
In nineteen sixty-eight, Erich von Däniken wrote that even two thousand men, working day and night, could never carve these figures out of the steel-hard volcanic stone with rudimentary tools.

22
00:01:52,533 --> 00:01:54,812
But the stone is not steel-hard.

23
00:01:54,812 --> 00:02:04,074
In the nineteen fifties, Rapa Nui carvers showed visiting archaeologists how it was done, with the old toki from the quarry floor.

24
00:02:04,074 --> 00:02:08,633
A medium statue, they estimated, took a small team about a year.

25
00:02:09,099 --> 00:02:12,806
The quarry also holds the biggest moai ever started.

26
00:02:12,806 --> 00:02:15,230
Archaeologists call it El Gigante.

27
00:02:15,230 --> 00:02:20,861
Twenty-one metres long, perhaps a hundred and sixty tonnes, and never cut free.

28
00:02:20,861 --> 00:02:25,066
It was never meant to move, or it was too ambitious to try.

29
00:02:25,533 --> 00:02:33,484
In twenty twenty-five, a team stitched eleven thousand drone photographs into a three-dimensional model of the quarry.

30
00:02:33,484 --> 00:02:41,300
They counted four hundred and twenty-six moai in progress, and about thirty separate workshops working side by side.

31
00:02:41,766 --> 00:02:43,448
No single king's factory.

32
00:02:43,448 --> 00:02:50,646
It looks like many family groups, each with its own corner of the mountain, each carving its own ancestors.

33
00:02:50,646 --> 00:02:52,933
That matters for the next station.

34
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:54,986
Station two: the descent.

35
00:02:54,986 --> 00:03:02,666
Once the keel was cut, the statue was lowered down the slope and stood upright in a pit, where carvers finished its back.

36
00:03:03,133 --> 00:03:10,687
Many of those statues are still standing there, buried to the shoulders by centuries of soil washing down the slope.

37
00:03:10,687 --> 00:03:14,400
That is why the world calls them the Easter Island heads.

38
00:03:14,866 --> 00:03:17,822
Dig around them and the heads have bodies.

39
00:03:17,822 --> 00:03:22,537
Torsos, long arms, hands with slender fingers resting on the belly.

40
00:03:22,537 --> 00:03:25,000
Some carry carvings on their backs.

41
00:03:25,466 --> 00:03:28,640
The half-finished quarry inspired another theory.

42
00:03:28,640 --> 00:03:38,033
In the eighteen hundreds some writers claimed the island was the last peak of a sunken continent, and that most of the statues lay under the sea.

43
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:40,746
There is no sunken continent.

44
00:03:40,746 --> 00:03:49,034
Rapa Nui is the top of volcanoes rising from deep ocean floor, and there is no evidence of drowned statues.

45
00:03:49,034 --> 00:03:52,133
The moai we know are the moai there are.

46
00:03:52,599 --> 00:03:54,324
So why so many unfinished?

47
00:03:54,324 --> 00:03:58,966
Some were simply abandoned when the carvers hit a lump of harder rock.

48
00:03:58,966 --> 00:04:02,215
Some were left mid-work when statue-making ended.

49
00:04:02,215 --> 00:04:05,133
And some may never have been meant to leave.

50
00:04:05,599 --> 00:04:08,372
But the finished ones had somewhere to be.

51
00:04:08,372 --> 00:04:13,388
Most were headed for stone platforms along the coast, often kilometres away.

52
00:04:13,388 --> 00:04:15,566
To get there, they took the road.

53
00:04:16,033 --> 00:04:17,692
Station three: the road.

54
00:04:17,692 --> 00:04:28,133
In nineteen fourteen, the British archaeologist Katherine Routledge noticed faint tracks radiating out from the quarry, with statues lying beside them.

55
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:30,546
Modern surveys measured them.

56
00:04:30,546 --> 00:04:35,311
The roads are about four and a half metres wide, and they are not flat.

57
00:04:35,311 --> 00:04:38,600
They are slightly concave, like a shallow trough.

58
00:04:39,066 --> 00:04:42,207
Along them lie the statues that never arrived.

59
00:04:42,207 --> 00:04:44,597
Archaeologists call them road moai.

60
00:04:44,597 --> 00:04:46,851
They are not scattered at random.

61
00:04:46,851 --> 00:04:53,133
The farther from the quarry, the fewer there are, as if each step carried a risk of falling.

62
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:55,613
And how they fell is telling.

63
00:04:55,613 --> 00:04:59,847
On stretches sloping uphill, they tend to lie on their backs.

64
00:04:59,847 --> 00:05:03,665
On downhill stretches, they tend to lie on their faces.

65
00:05:03,665 --> 00:05:07,899
That is how an upright object falls, not one lying on a sled.

66
00:05:08,366 --> 00:05:18,769
In twenty twenty-five, Carl Lipo and Terry Hunt published measurements of nine hundred and sixty-two moai, sixty-two of them abandoned on the roads.

67
00:05:18,769 --> 00:05:21,300
The road moai are built differently.

68
00:05:21,766 --> 00:05:25,684
Their bases are wide and D-shaped, rounded at the front.

69
00:05:25,684 --> 00:05:29,953
And they lean forward, between about six and fifteen degrees.

70
00:05:29,953 --> 00:05:34,571
So far forward that their centre of mass sits over the front edge.

71
00:05:34,571 --> 00:05:37,299
A road moai cannot stand up on its own.

72
00:05:37,766 --> 00:05:42,492
For a statue meant to be dragged on its back, that shape makes no sense.

73
00:05:42,492 --> 00:05:47,021
For a statue meant to travel upright, it is exactly the right design.

74
00:05:47,021 --> 00:05:48,399
The lean is built in.

75
00:05:48,866 --> 00:05:50,394
And here is the twist.

76
00:05:50,394 --> 00:05:55,882
Statues that reached their platforms had their bases recut, to remove the lean.

77
00:05:55,882 --> 00:06:00,466
Once the journey was over, the walking shape was no longer needed.

78
00:06:00,933 --> 00:06:02,539
Station four: the walk.

79
00:06:02,539 --> 00:06:07,216
Outsiders have been testing transport ideas here for seventy years.

80
00:06:07,216 --> 00:06:13,290
In nineteen fifty-six, Thor Heyerdahl's expedition tied a ten-tonne statue to a sledge.

81
00:06:13,290 --> 00:06:17,199
It took about a hundred and eighty islanders to pull it.

82
00:06:17,666 --> 00:06:23,895
In nineteen sixty, William Mulloy proposed a rocking sledge with giant wooden legs.

83
00:06:23,895 --> 00:06:30,199
Ingenious, but it needed big timber, and many road moai lack the belly it relied on.

84
00:06:30,666 --> 00:06:37,498
Then, in nineteen eighty-two, a Czech engineer named Pavel Pavel took the old stories literally.

85
00:06:37,498 --> 00:06:45,966
In a field in Czechoslovakia, seventeen people with ropes rocked a twelve-tonne concrete replica forward, step by step.

86
00:06:46,433 --> 00:06:53,755
In nineteen eighty-six he tried it on Rapa Nui with Heyerdahl, on real moai of five and nine tonnes.

87
00:06:53,755 --> 00:06:54,487
It worked.

88
00:06:54,487 --> 00:06:59,833
But the twisting motion chipped the statues' bases, and they had to stop.

89
00:07:00,300 --> 00:07:03,401
Others tried upright sleds on log rollers.

90
00:07:03,401 --> 00:07:14,699
In nineteen ninety-eight, Jo Anne Van Tilburg's team laid a replica flat on a wooden sledge, and sixty Rapa Nui people hauled it almost a hundred metres.

91
00:07:15,166 --> 00:07:17,445
Every method could move a statue.

92
00:07:17,445 --> 00:07:20,691
The question was which one fitted the evidence.

93
00:07:20,691 --> 00:07:34,366
In twenty eleven, Lipo and Hunt, working with Rapa Nui archaeologist Sergio Rapu Haoa, built a replica of an actual road moai: three metres tall, four point three five tonnes, with the forward lean.

94
00:07:34,833 --> 00:07:37,344
They tied three ropes to its head.

95
00:07:37,344 --> 00:07:42,662
Two teams on the sides, one team behind to keep it from tipping forward.

96
00:07:42,662 --> 00:07:43,918
Then they pulled.

97
00:07:43,918 --> 00:07:44,287
Left.

98
00:07:44,287 --> 00:07:44,730
Right.

99
00:07:44,730 --> 00:07:45,099
Left.

100
00:07:45,566 --> 00:07:51,918
With each sideways tilt, the statue pivoted on the rounded front of its base and swung forward.

101
00:07:51,918 --> 00:07:52,653
It waddled.

102
00:07:52,653 --> 00:07:57,066
Eighteen people walked it a hundred metres in about forty minutes.

103
00:07:57,533 --> 00:08:01,210
Physically, a leaning moai is an inverted pendulum.

104
00:08:01,210 --> 00:08:04,960
The lean turns sideways rocking into forward motion.

105
00:08:04,960 --> 00:08:11,233
And a concave road holds the base steady while it rocks, like a groove guiding a wheel.

106
00:08:11,699 --> 00:08:13,011
No forest of logs.

107
00:08:13,011 --> 00:08:14,687
No hundreds of haulers.

108
00:08:14,687 --> 00:08:18,331
A few dozen people with rope, practice and rhythm.

109
00:08:18,331 --> 00:08:24,233
Lipo's line is that every time they moved a statue, they were also making a road.

110
00:08:24,699 --> 00:08:26,433
Station five: the words.

111
00:08:26,433 --> 00:08:29,033
Because none of this was really new.

112
00:08:29,033 --> 00:08:34,666
Rapa Nui people had been telling visitors for centuries how the statues moved.

113
00:08:35,133 --> 00:08:40,223
The stories say the moai walked, carried by mana, a sacred power.

114
00:08:40,223 --> 00:08:46,566
The earliest versions credit a king named Tuu Ku Ihu, helped by the god Makemake.

115
00:08:47,033 --> 00:08:50,474
In nineteen fourteen, Routledge wrote down another.

116
00:08:50,474 --> 00:08:58,233
An old woman, cook to the image-makers at the mountain, moved the statues by mana, ordering them about at her will.

117
00:08:58,700 --> 00:09:05,552
There is even a Rapa Nui phrase for it, neke neke, often explained as walking without legs.

118
00:09:05,552 --> 00:09:09,166
It turns up in old chants and children's rhymes.

119
00:09:09,633 --> 00:09:13,264
For decades, outsiders treated this as pure myth.

120
00:09:13,264 --> 00:09:17,413
Or worse, as a garbled memory of something otherworldly.

121
00:09:17,413 --> 00:09:19,784
Statues that move by themselves?

122
00:09:19,784 --> 00:09:21,933
Surely that meant levitation.

123
00:09:22,399 --> 00:09:25,300
But put the legend next to the experiment.

124
00:09:25,300 --> 00:09:31,516
A standing statue, rocking side to side, appearing to stride forward with no visible legs.

125
00:09:31,516 --> 00:09:34,900
The people who watched it described exactly that.

126
00:09:35,366 --> 00:09:38,773
The oral tradition was not a riddle about aliens.

127
00:09:38,773 --> 00:09:45,933
It was an eyewitness description, kept for generations, that outsiders were too slow to take literally.

128
00:09:46,399 --> 00:09:49,275
Station six: the ahu, the stone platform.

129
00:09:49,275 --> 00:09:57,833
Most ahu stand near the coast, and most moai stand with their backs to the sea, facing inland, watching over their people.

130
00:09:58,299 --> 00:10:01,985
Raising a statue on its platform did not need cranes.

131
00:10:01,985 --> 00:10:12,833
In the nineteen fifties, islanders demonstrated it with wooden levers, prying the statue up a little at a time and packing stones underneath until it stood.

132
00:10:13,299 --> 00:10:16,704
Once standing, a moai could receive its eyes.

133
00:10:16,704 --> 00:10:26,766
In nineteen seventy-nine, Sergio Rapu Haoa's team found fragments of eyes made of white coral, with pupils of obsidian or red scoria.

134
00:10:27,233 --> 00:10:35,046
Some statues also wear a pukao, a huge cylinder of red volcanic scoria from a second quarry, Puna Pau.

135
00:10:35,046 --> 00:10:38,033
The biggest weigh around twelve tonnes.

136
00:10:38,500 --> 00:10:49,131
A twenty eighteen study suggests they were rolled across the island, then rolled up an earth ramp against the leaning statue, using a rope trick called parbuckling.

137
00:10:49,131 --> 00:10:51,399
Perhaps fifteen people were enough.

138
00:10:51,866 --> 00:11:01,577
The largest platform, Ahu Tongariki, holds fifteen moai, one of them about eighty-six tonnes, the heaviest ever raised on the island.

139
00:11:01,577 --> 00:11:05,666
The tallest ever erected, Paro, stood almost ten metres.

140
00:11:06,133 --> 00:11:10,434
These were not idols for visitors, or beacons for spaceships.

141
00:11:10,434 --> 00:11:15,933
Each moai was an ancestor, carrying mana for the family whose land it watched.

142
00:11:16,399 --> 00:11:18,050
Station seven: the fall.

143
00:11:18,050 --> 00:11:24,171
When Dutch ships arrived in seventeen twenty-two, statues still stood on their platforms.

144
00:11:24,171 --> 00:11:28,366
By eighteen sixty-eight, not one remained standing on an ahu.

145
00:11:28,833 --> 00:11:30,887
Why they fell is still argued.

146
00:11:30,887 --> 00:11:36,295
Oral accounts mention rival groups pulling statues down, and the earth shaking.

147
00:11:36,295 --> 00:11:39,033
Many fell forward and broke at the neck.

148
00:11:39,500 --> 00:11:43,628
And there was a real catastrophe, but it came from outside.

149
00:11:43,628 --> 00:11:50,974
In eighteen sixty-two, Peruvian slave raiders took about fifteen hundred people, roughly half the island.

150
00:11:50,974 --> 00:11:53,633
The few who returned brought smallpox.

151
00:11:54,100 --> 00:11:58,899
The famous story of an island that destroyed itself also took a hit.

152
00:11:58,899 --> 00:12:05,251
In twenty twenty-four, ancient genomes showed no population crash in the sixteen hundreds.

153
00:12:05,251 --> 00:12:09,133
The population kept growing until the eighteen sixties.

154
00:12:09,600 --> 00:12:15,636
The forests did disappear, cleared for farming while introduced rats ate the palm seeds.

155
00:12:15,636 --> 00:12:21,466
But islanders adapted, with rock gardens and the sea, and kept carving for centuries.

156
00:12:21,933 --> 00:12:24,217
Even the statues travelled again.

157
00:12:24,217 --> 00:12:29,822
In eighteen sixty-eight a British ship carried off a moai called Hoa Hakananai'a.

158
00:12:29,822 --> 00:12:34,666
It is still in the British Museum, and Rapa Nui has asked for it back.

159
00:12:35,133 --> 00:12:39,968
In nineteen sixty, a tsunami swept the Tongariki statues inland.

160
00:12:39,968 --> 00:12:46,086
In the nineteen nineties, archaeologists and islanders stood all fifteen back up.

161
00:12:46,086 --> 00:12:47,899
This time, with a crane.

162
00:12:48,366 --> 00:12:49,323
End of the road.

163
00:12:49,323 --> 00:12:50,700
Time for the scorecard.

164
00:12:51,166 --> 00:12:55,113
Carved by Rapa Nui people with stone tools: evidence.

165
00:12:55,113 --> 00:12:58,613
Road moai shaped for upright walking: evidence.

166
00:12:58,613 --> 00:13:02,635
Eighteen people walked a four-tonne replica: evidence.

167
00:13:02,635 --> 00:13:06,433
The oral tradition that the moai walked: supported.

168
00:13:06,899 --> 00:13:10,535
Stranded aliens with advanced tools: no evidence.

169
00:13:10,535 --> 00:13:15,432
A sunken continent, and an island that destroyed itself: debunked.

170
00:13:15,432 --> 00:13:20,033
The exact method on every road and every slope: still debated.

171
00:13:20,500 --> 00:13:21,299
The verdict.

172
00:13:21,299 --> 00:13:24,299
The alien explanation fails at every station.

173
00:13:24,299 --> 00:13:28,100
Not because it is too bold, but because it is not needed.

174
00:13:28,100 --> 00:13:30,700
The statues tell us how they travelled.

175
00:13:30,700 --> 00:13:35,433
Their shape, their roads, and the way they fell all point the same way.

176
00:13:35,899 --> 00:13:37,954
What remains open is detail.

177
00:13:37,954 --> 00:13:44,337
How many statues walked, how many rode on sledges, and how the very largest ever moved.

178
00:13:44,337 --> 00:13:49,399
Archaeology answers that with more measurement, not with more aliens.

179
00:13:49,866 --> 00:13:53,868
And the people who knew the answer all along are still there.

180
00:13:53,868 --> 00:13:55,770
The moai are their ancestors.

181
00:13:55,770 --> 00:14:02,133
The road from the quarry is their history, and the experiment only caught up with what they said.

182
00:14:02,600 --> 00:14:04,323
That's the end of the road.

183
00:14:04,323 --> 00:14:06,303
Do you think every moai walked?

184
00:14:06,303 --> 00:14:11,666
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
